eng'd J1 and T6, so all melds are exposed. Cannot get Fully Concealed Hand (4 pts) or Melded Hand (6 pts requires *all* melds from others).\n\nBut we *can* still aim for **Self Drawn** (1 pt) and **Two Dragon Pungs** (6 pts \u2014 we have one dragon pung already; need another? No \u2014 we only have J1). Wait: we have only one dragon pung.\n\nWait: Do we have more than one? Only [J1,J1,J1]. So only one dragon pung.\n\nCurrent score contributors:\n- All Pungs (if final hand is four pungs/kongs + pair): 6 pts\n- Two Dragon Pungs? No \u2014 only one dragon pung \u2192 cannot count\n- Dragon Pung: Yes \u2192 2 pts\n- Prevalent Wind (F4 pung/pair): we don\u2019t have a *pung* of F4, only a *pair*. So **no** \u2014 pung/kong required. Pattern says: \"A hand with a pung or kong of the prevalent wind\". We don't have a pung of F4 \u2192 so **Prevalent Wind** does **not** count.\n- Similarly, **Seat Wind**: same condition \u2014 must be a pung/kong \u2192 we only have a pair \u2192 does **not** count.\n- Therefore, holding F4 pair gives **zero** direct points unless we promote it to a kong? Even then, it would give the pattern.\n\nWait: correction! The pattern says: \u201cPung of the prevalent/seat wind\u201d \u2014 so **must be a pung or kong**, not a pair. So having a pair of F4 gives **no points** for those patterns.\n\nHowever, if we keep F4 and later get a third from draw, we could Peng and form a pung \u2192 then gain both Prevalent Wind and Seat Wind (since North = both), worth 2+2=4 pts.\n\nAlso, currently, our valid actions include **keeping F4** (by discarding something else).\n\nSo key strategic choice: **keep F4 pair for potential future pung of North Wind (F4)** to gain 4 points.\n\nNow evaluate which discard minimizes loss of potential while avoiding danger.\n\nT1 x2: Could form chow with T2,T3 \u2014 but T2 is low, T3 is being used by others. Across and previous players both used T3,T4,T5 \u2014 very likely someone is building T3-T4-T5 chows. So T1 might be safer than T2.\n\nB9: Terminal \u2014 could be useful in outside hand, but we lack terminals elsewhere. W6 is simple, T6 is simple, etc. Not clearly heading toward Outside Hand.\n\nW6: Mid-game number, fairly safe.\n\nB6: Middle tile, also fairly safe.\n\nBut best move is to **preserve flexibility**.\n\nWe have:\n- Two identical pungs: J1 (dragon), T6 (simple)\n- Possible direction: **All Pungs** (6 pts) + **Dragon Pung** (2 pts) + possibly **Mixed Shifted Pungs** or **Triple Pung**?\n\nCheck:\n- Triple Pung: three pungs of same number in different suits. We have T6 pung. If we had W6 or B6 pung \u2192 possible. But we only have single W6 and B6 \u2014 unlikely.\n- Mixed Shifted Pungs: three pungs in different suits with consecutive numbers? E.g., 5-6-7 type. We have T6. Could go for W5/W7? But W7-W8-W9 already melded by next player. W5 unshown: 3 \u2014 possible. But hard.\n\nAlternatively, consider **Half Flush**? Our melds are J1 (honor), T6 (bamboo), and possible F4 pung (wind). If we convert hand into mostly bamboo + honors, possible.\n\nCurrent hand: T1,T1,T2,B6,B9,W6,F4,F4\n\nBut we have too many suit types.\n\nBetter idea: focus on **exposed, high-probability win via All Pungs (6 pts)** + **Dragon Pung (2 pts)** + if we get F4 pung \u2192 **Prevalent & Seat Wind (2+2)** \u2192 total 10 pts minimum.\n\nPlus possibly **Self Drawn (1)** \u2192 11, or **Melded Kong (1)** if we Gang later.\n\nCurrently, we can only win if we reach at least 8 pts.\n\nSo core plan: preserve F4 pair for potential pung, avoid breaking up possible future pungs.\n\nNow assess safety:\n\n- **Play T1**: We have two. Discarding one keeps a pair. Others are playing T3-T5 \u2014 T1 less likely to complete chow immediately. Safer.\n- **Play T2**: Only 2 unshown; across player just discarded T2 \u2014 suggests they don\u2019t need it, but maybe they completed a set. Still, T1-T2-T3 is common \u2014 risky.\n- **Play B9**: Terminal \u2014 always somewhat risky, but no one has shown B9 interest. Previous player discarded B9 \u2014 suggests not needed. Possibly safe.\n- **Play W6**: W6 unshown: 2. Next player has W7-W8-W9 \u2014 they might want W6 for extended wait? But they already have full meld. Unlikely. Fairly safe.\n- **Play B6**: B6 unshown: 3. No one has shown B6 interest. Safe.\n- **Play F4**: Dangerous strategically \u2014 throws away chance for 4-point wind patterns. And F4 only has 2 unshown \u2014 not abundant. Risky to discard.\n\nThus, safest productive move is to discard **one of the duplicates that doesn\u2019t harm future plans**.\n\nWe have **two T1s** \u2014 best candidate. Discard one T1.\n\nWhy not others?\n- F4: Keep for wind pung potential.\n- B9: Terminal \u2014 harder to use, but could help Outside Hand? Not likely given rest of hand.\n- T2: Single copy \u2014 and complements T1s. Better to keep.\n- W6/B6: Singles, mid-number \u2014 neutral.\n\nSo best discard: **Play T1**\n\nReason:  \nDiscarding T1 minimizes risk to our strategic goals. We retain the F4 pair for potential Prevalent Wind and Seat Wind patterns (each worth 2 points) if we can form a pung later. T1 is relatively safe as other players are focused on mid-bamboo sequences (T3-T5), and we hold no strong bamboo chow prospects. Keeping flexibility while aiming for All Pungs (6 pts) + Dragon Pung (2 pts) + possible wind pung offers a clear path to 8+ points.\n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
