emaining single tiles: W3, W6, T2, B6, B7. Among these, B6 and B7 are adjacent, possibly useful for a future Chow, while T2 is isolated. W3 and W6 are spaced.\\n\\nStill, forming the Chow improves structure. With three established melds (J1 Kong, T6 Pung, T7-T8-T9 Chow), you\\u2019re one meld away from completion. Potential final melds could be a pair (e.g., if you draw another T2, B6, B7, W3, or W6) or extend existing sequences.\\n\\nAlso note: Your seat wind and prevailing wind are both **North (F4)**. However, you have no F4 tiles, and all F4s have been discarded (unshown count = 0). So **Seat Wind** and **Prevalent Wind** patterns (each worth 2 points) are unattainable.\\n\\nMoreover, honor tiles are largely gone \\u2014 J1 is konged, J2 is low, J3 all gone; winds all discarded. So hands like All Honors or Big Four Winds are impossible.\\n\\nYou currently have:\\n- One exposed Kong (J1)\\n- One exposed Pung (T6)\\n- Now attempting to form a Chow\\n\\nThis leans toward a **Melded Hand** (6 points), which requires all melds to be formed by other players' discards and the winning tile to come from another player. That\\u2019s consistent with your path.\\n\\nEven better: You may aim for **Half Flush** (6 points): all tiles from one suit + honors. Currently, you have many W tiles (W3, W6), plus T6, T7, T8, T9, B6, B7 \\u2014 too mixed. But if you form T7-T8-T9, and keep T6, you can go for a **Tong-its-style** bamboo-based hand? Wait \\u2014 your T6 pung is in **Dots (B)**? No \\u2014 wait: **T** = Bamboo (\\u6761), so T6 = Bamboo-6.\\n\\nWait \\u2014 correction: In our notation:\\n- W = Characters (\\u4e07)\\n- T = Bamboos (\\u6761)\\n- B = Dots (\\u997c)\\n\\nSo T6 = Bamboo-6, T7 = Bamboo-7, T8 = Bamboo-8, T9 = Bamboo-9.\\n\\nYou have:\\n- Meld: [T6, T6, T6] \\u2192 Bamboo-6 Pung\\n- Hand: T7, T9 \\u2192 Bamboo-7 and Bamboo-9\\n- Claiming T8 \\u2192 forms [T7, T8, T9] \\u2192 Bamboo-7,8,9 Chow\\n\\nSo now you have two Bamboo melds: T666 and T789. That\\u2019s strong